Now I understand the full picture. The `render()` function in `js/app.js` destroys all `<li>` elements on every render by setting `todoList.innerHTML = ''` (line 80) and recreating them. This is the root cause of all 9 failures.

The 9 failing targets are all on the same seam cluster - the `render()` function. All the DOM identity scenarios fail because the captured element references become disconnected from the DOM when `render()` is called. The source-level conformance scenario fails because it detects `todoList.innerHTML = ''` on line 80.

The fix is to refactor `render()` to mutate existing `<li>` nodes in place instead of destroying and recreating them. This is the smallest production change needed.

Let me think about the approach:

1. Instead of `todoList.innerHTML = ''`, I need to build a map of existing `<li>` elements by todo ID, then for each todo:
   - If the todo already has an `<li>`, update it in place (toggle class names, update checkbox state, update label text, etc.)
   - If the todo is new, create a new `<li>`
   - Remove `<li>` elements that don't correspond to any todo

2. For the source-level conformance check, I need to remove the `todoList.innerHTML = ''` assignment.

Let me think about the implementation more carefully.

The current render() function:
1. Gets todos from localStorage
2. Filters them
3. Sets `todoList.innerHTML = ''`
4. For each todo, creates a new `<li>` and appends it
5. Adds event listeners on each `<li>`
6. Updates main/footer visibility, toggle-all, clear-completed, todo-count, filter links

The new approach:
1. Gets todos from localStorage
2. Filters them
3. Creates a map of existing `<li>` elements keyed by todo ID
4. For each filtered todo, either update existing `<li>` or create new one
5. Remove `<li>` elements that are no longer needed
6. Update main/footer visibility, toggle-all, clear-completed, todo-count, filter links

The tricky part is that the current code stores event listeners on the DOM elements. I need to maintain the event listeners. Let me think about how to structure this.

Actually, looking at the code more carefully, the event listeners are added during render() on each `<li>`. So I need to either:
a) Re-attach event listeners on updated elements (which is fine, they're the same element)
b) Find the existing `<li>` by matching todo, update its content, and re-attach event listeners

Let me design the refactor:

```js
function render() {
    todos = getTodos();
    var filtered = getFilteredTodos();

    // Build a map of existing <li> elements by todo ID
    var existingItems = {};
    var listItems = todoList.children;
    for (var i = 0; i < listItems.length; i++) {
      var li = listItems[i];
      var id = li.getAttribute('data-id');
      if (id) existingItems[id] = li;
    }

    // Track which IDs we've seen
    var seen = {};

    filtered.forEach(function (todo) {
      var li = existingItems[todo.id];
      if (!li) {
        li = document.createElement('li');
        li.setAttribute('data-id', todo.id);
        li.innerHTML = 
          '<div class="view">' +
            '<input class="toggle" type="checkbox">' +
            '<label></label>' +
            '<button class="destroy"></button>' +
          '</div>' +
          '<input class="edit">';
        todoList.appendChild(li);
      }

      // Update the element in place
      li.className = todo.completed ? 'completed' : '';
      if (todo.editing) li.classList.add('editing');

      var toggle = li.querySelector('.toggle');
      toggle.checked = todo.completed;

      var label = li.querySelector('label');
      label.textContent = todo.title;

      var editInput = li.querySelector('.edit');
      editInput.value = todo.title;

      // ... event listeners (only if new, or re-attach)
      // Actually, we need to be careful about event listeners
    });

    // Remove items that no longer exist
    // ...
}
```

Actually, the issue is that event listeners need to be re-attached if we want to keep the approach simple. Let me think about the smallest change.

The simplest approach:
1. Instead of `todoList.innerHTML = ''`, use a loop to remove extra children
2. For each todo, find or create the `<li>` and update its content
3. Keep the event listeners attached by re-attaching them or by using event delegation

Actually, thinking about it more carefully, the simplest approach is to NOT destroy the `<li>` elements. Instead, I can:

1. Keep a map of `<li>` elements by todo ID
2. For each todo in the filtered list, reuse or create the `<li>` and update its content
3. Remove `<li>` elements that are no longer in the filtered list
4. Re-attach event listeners on the reused elements

Let me look at this from the smallest change perspective. The current code does:

```js
todoList.innerHTML = '';
filtered.forEach(function (todo) {
    var li = document.createElement('li');
    // ... set up li ...
    todoList.appendChild(li);
});
```

The smallest change is to replace this with an in-place mutation approach:

```js
// Remove any existing items that shouldn't be in the filtered list
var existingItems = todoList.children;
var existingIds = [];
for (var i = 0; i < existingItems.length; i++) {
    existingIds.push(existingItems[i].getAttribute('data-id'));
}

// Remove items not in filtered list
var filteredIds = filtered.map(function(t) { return t.id; });
for (var i = existingItems.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    if (filteredIds.indexOf(existingItems[i].getAttribute('data-id')) === -1) {
        todoList.removeChild(existingItems[i]);
    }
}

filtered.forEach(function (todo) {
    var li = todoList.querySelector('li[data-id="' + todo.id + '"]');
    if (!li) {
        li = document.createElement('li');
        li.setAttribute('data-id', todo.id);
        // ... set innerHTML for the initial structure ...
        todoList.appendChild(li);
    }

    // Update in place
    // toggle class, update checkbox, update label, etc.
});
```

But this is getting complex. Let me think about what's the simplest approach.

Actually, the simplest approach that preserves element identity is to use a keyed approach:

1. Give each `<li>` a `data-id` attribute
2. Instead of clearing `todoList.innerHTML`, iterate over the filtered todos and reuse existing `<li>` elements by matching on `data-id`
3. Remove `<li>` elements that don't match any todo
4. Update the content of each `<li>` in place

Let me also look at the steps.js to understand the step definitions for the DOM identity scenarios.

